00:00:04.996 --> 00:00:06.620
SPEAKER: When building
apps, developers

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:10.250
rely on various cloud services
including storage, messaging,

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.680
data analytics, and
mobile development.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.500
As developers expand their
use, seamless integration

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.260
between these services
can be a real challenge.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.780
Google Cloud Functions is
a server list execution

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.930
environment for building and
connecting cloud services.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.750
With Cloud Functions, you
write simple, single-purpose

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.420
functions that are attached to
events emitted from your cloud

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.280
infrastructure and services.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.220
Your Cloud Function is triggered
when an event being watched

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.310
is fired.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.550
Cloud events are
things that happen

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.170
in your cloud environment.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.350
These might be things like
changes to data in a database,

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.560
files added to a storage
system, or a new virtual machine

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.180
instance being created.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.460
Events occur whether or not
you choose to respond to them.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.010
You create a response to
an event with a trigger.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.830
A trigger is a
declaration that you

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.860
are interested in a certain
event or set of events.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.040
Finding a function to a trigger
allows you to capture and act

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.030
on events.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.790
For more information on creating
triggers and associating them

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:08.450
with your functions, see events
and triggers linked below.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:11.570
Your code executes in a
fully managed environment.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.210
There is no need to
provision any infrastructure

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.910
or worry about
managing any servers.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.900
This hands-on lab shows you
how to create, deploy, and test

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.540
a Cloud Function using
Google Cloud Console.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.140
So what you'll do in this lab is
create a Google Cloud Function,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.230
deploy and test the
function, view the logs.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.930
By the end, you will have
deployed, tested, and viewed

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.100
the logs of your application.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.300
Next up on our quest is Google
Cloud Pub/Sub Qwik Start

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.850
Console.

